1
00:00:01,210 --> 00:00:03,210
At this year's
festival selection,

2
00:00:03,290 --> 00:00:05,040
I'd like our workshop
to take part.

3
00:00:06,290 --> 00:00:08,380
If we do, it might help us
return to our former--

4
00:00:08,460 --> 00:00:09,290
[GLEN]
No.

5
00:00:09,380 --> 00:00:10,210
Huh?

6
00:00:10,630 --> 00:00:12,170
We will not participate.

7
00:00:13,250 --> 00:00:14,460
But, sir...

8
00:00:15,920 --> 00:00:17,290
I don't understand.

9
00:00:17,380 --> 00:00:19,880
It's an opportunity
to rebuild the workshop.

10
00:00:19,960 --> 00:00:21,540
[GLEN]
The last master refused.

11
00:00:21,630 --> 00:00:23,500
But why?

12
00:00:23,580 --> 00:00:26,080
It was us who originally
supplied the designs

13
00:00:26,170 --> 00:00:28,420
for the Holy Beginnings Festival

14
00:00:28,500 --> 00:00:32,250
until the previous monarch
changed the policies around it.

15
00:00:32,330 --> 00:00:35,880
The selections began,
and we were never once chosen.

16
00:00:36,540 --> 00:00:39,040
You see, the last master
of the Paige Workshop

17
00:00:39,130 --> 00:00:40,250
was my father.

18
00:00:40,330 --> 00:00:41,960
It was his choice.

19
00:00:42,710 --> 00:00:44,580
The royal family
and the kingdom's religion

20
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:45,630
look down on us,

21
00:00:45,710 --> 00:00:47,670
and this was his response
to that.

22
00:00:47,750 --> 00:00:49,580
It's not my place to change it.

23
00:00:49,670 --> 00:00:52,080
Why did they never choose
the Paige Workshop?

24
00:00:52,170 --> 00:00:53,880
This conversation is over.

25
00:00:56,710 --> 00:00:58,130
Then excuse me.

26
00:00:59,420 --> 00:01:01,880
[door opens and closes]

27
00:01:05,290 --> 00:01:06,670
[door opens]

28
00:02:40,670 --> 00:02:42,290
[ANNE]
There has to be more to it.

29
00:02:42,380 --> 00:02:44,330
There's some reason
why the Paige Workshop

30
00:02:44,420 --> 00:02:45,920
has never been selected.

31
00:02:46,750 --> 00:02:49,670
And right now,
our shop's missing something.

32
00:02:49,750 --> 00:02:51,040
If you let us participate,

33
00:02:51,130 --> 00:02:53,330
I think we can figure out
what it is.

34
00:02:54,080 --> 00:02:56,580
I cannot change
my father's policies.

35
00:02:58,250 --> 00:02:59,750
Not only that,

36
00:02:59,830 --> 00:03:02,130
the legacy
that we have inherited

37
00:03:02,210 --> 00:03:04,000
must not be disrespected.

38
00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:06,540
But, sir,

39
00:03:06,630 --> 00:03:08,630
what if the previous masters
were mistaken

40
00:03:08,710 --> 00:03:09,920
in their decisions?

41
00:03:10,670 --> 00:03:12,000
Mistaken?

42
00:03:12,080 --> 00:03:14,130
Are you saying
they led us astray?

43
00:03:14,210 --> 00:03:15,920
That they failed?

44
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:17,830
How dare you, Anne?

45
00:03:18,250 --> 00:03:20,750
I believe
that everyone makes mistakes,

46
00:03:20,830 --> 00:03:23,210
and it isn't something
to be ashamed of.

47
00:03:23,290 --> 00:03:25,380
Do you realize
what you're saying?

48
00:03:25,460 --> 00:03:28,880
I refuse to sit by and allow you
to insult our workshop.

49
00:03:29,830 --> 00:03:32,710
If you claim that we're
perpetuating past mistakes,

50
00:03:32,790 --> 00:03:34,580
then you're suggesting
that our traditions

51
00:03:34,670 --> 00:03:35,920
are mistakes.

52
00:03:38,880 --> 00:03:40,960
[ANNE]
I'm sorry if that insulted you,

53
00:03:41,040 --> 00:03:43,420
but right now,
you're insulting me.

54
00:03:43,500 --> 00:03:44,580
Pardon?

55
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:48,540
You've appointed me as the Head
of Artisans here at Paige

56
00:03:48,630 --> 00:03:51,080
yet when you defended
your position just then,

57
00:03:51,170 --> 00:03:52,960
you said "our workshop"

58
00:03:53,040 --> 00:03:55,710
as though you believe
that I'm not a part of it.

59
00:03:55,790 --> 00:03:57,580
But the truth is, I am.

60
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:00,130
Technically, you're not one
of our artisans, Anne.

61
00:04:00,210 --> 00:04:03,290
You're wrong.
You invited me to join you.

62
00:04:03,380 --> 00:04:05,750
You said that
you had high hopes for me,

63
00:04:05,830 --> 00:04:09,080
with responsibility
as a silver sugar master.

64
00:04:09,170 --> 00:04:12,170
So I'm going to fulfill
those responsibilities.

65
00:04:12,250 --> 00:04:16,000
Whatever it takes,
I will rebuild this place.

66
00:04:16,670 --> 00:04:18,710
Because that is my job.

67
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:22,330
[laughs]

68
00:04:22,420 --> 00:04:23,670
Elliott?

69
00:04:23,750 --> 00:04:25,580
It's nothing at all, I'm sorry.

70
00:04:25,670 --> 00:04:27,750
Allow me
to escort her away now, sir.

71
00:04:27,830 --> 00:04:29,040
If this goes any further,

72
00:04:29,130 --> 00:04:32,080
I'm afraid she may cause you
to have an attack.

73
00:04:32,170 --> 00:04:33,540
But I still have things to say.

74
00:04:33,630 --> 00:04:36,080
Let's go, you can discuss it
another time.

75
00:04:38,250 --> 00:04:39,290
[door closes]

76
00:04:40,580 --> 00:04:43,080
[ELLIOTT]
My, my, what a spectacle!

77
00:04:43,170 --> 00:04:46,420
To think you would defy
Mr. Glen himself.

78
00:04:46,500 --> 00:04:48,960
I never thought
I would witness such a thing.

79
00:04:49,040 --> 00:04:50,380
You know, if you disagree

80
00:04:50,460 --> 00:04:52,330
with the way Mr. Glen
handles things,

81
00:04:52,420 --> 00:04:54,880
I'm sure you could
say something yourself.

82
00:04:54,960 --> 00:04:56,670
Assuming that I disagreed.

83
00:04:56,750 --> 00:04:57,920
Huh?

84
00:04:58,000 --> 00:05:00,580
The truth is, everyone
who's still at the workshop

85
00:05:00,670 --> 00:05:03,710
either feels a deep sense
of gratitude for Mr. Glen

86
00:05:03,790 --> 00:05:06,630
or at least a strong admiration
for him.

87
00:05:07,290 --> 00:05:08,540
My mother used to be a cook here

88
00:05:08,630 --> 00:05:10,580
at the main house
back in the day.

89
00:05:10,670 --> 00:05:12,420
She raised me
and my older sister

90
00:05:12,500 --> 00:05:14,000
all by herself.

91
00:05:14,920 --> 00:05:16,790
But when I was seven years old,

92
00:05:16,880 --> 00:05:19,710
she ended up sick
and wasn't able to work anymore.

93
00:05:19,790 --> 00:05:22,420
The one who lent a helping hand
in our time of need

94
00:05:22,500 --> 00:05:23,960
was old Mr. Glen.

95
00:05:24,710 --> 00:05:26,380
I was too little to do much,

96
00:05:26,460 --> 00:05:28,250
but he took me on
as an apprentice

97
00:05:28,330 --> 00:05:30,210
so that I could earn some wages.

98
00:05:30,920 --> 00:05:33,170
The others have a similar story.

99
00:05:33,250 --> 00:05:35,420
All of us are here
because of Mr. Glen.

100
00:05:35,500 --> 00:05:37,580
- [ANNE] Really?
- Yes, really.

101
00:05:38,460 --> 00:05:42,580
Pardon, could you spare us
a moment of your time, please?

102
00:05:45,290 --> 00:05:46,670
[ORLAND]
That should fix it.

103
00:05:46,750 --> 00:05:48,210
Great, thanks.

104
00:05:48,290 --> 00:05:50,540
We'll hurry
and get your meals ready.

105
00:05:50,630 --> 00:05:52,830
I'm sorry to bother you
while you're working.

106
00:05:52,920 --> 00:05:55,330
If you were sorry,
you wouldn't have done it.

107
00:05:55,420 --> 00:05:58,130
But you're the one in charge
of maintaining it, aren't you?

108
00:05:58,210 --> 00:05:59,290
[Orland sighs]

109
00:05:59,380 --> 00:06:02,540
Honestly, you've been this way
since you were a child.

110
00:06:02,630 --> 00:06:05,420
Oh, you've been here
for just as long?

111
00:06:05,500 --> 00:06:06,830
Yes. Orland and I

112
00:06:06,920 --> 00:06:09,830
have quite a storied history,
don't we?

113
00:06:09,920 --> 00:06:12,750
My father was an artisan here
years ago.

114
00:06:12,830 --> 00:06:14,000
After he passed,

115
00:06:14,080 --> 00:06:17,210
Mr. Glen generously took me in,
that's why.

116
00:06:17,290 --> 00:06:19,750
So, you know,
Orland never cuts his hair short

117
00:06:19,830 --> 00:06:21,960
because he made a wish
a while back.

118
00:06:22,040 --> 00:06:25,380
He hoped for Mr. Glen to be
cured of his illness someday.

119
00:06:25,460 --> 00:06:26,580
Quiet.

120
00:06:26,670 --> 00:06:28,330
[KING]
Are you two fighting in here?

121
00:06:29,250 --> 00:06:31,880
Ugh, I'm really hungry.

122
00:06:31,960 --> 00:06:34,460
Uh-oh, now the big
scary one's here, too.

123
00:06:36,500 --> 00:06:38,250
[VALENTINE]
King used to be a ruffian.

124
00:06:38,330 --> 00:06:40,500
He was so tough,
no one would cross him.

125
00:06:41,080 --> 00:06:43,130
[NADIR] But Mr. Glen went
and hired him anyway.

126
00:06:43,210 --> 00:06:45,960
Hm. That's ancient history.

127
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:47,670
I suppose
it was a little similar

128
00:06:47,750 --> 00:06:50,880
to the way Nadir and I started
our employment at Paige as well.

129
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:53,710
If I'm remembering correctly,
Mr. Glen brought you onboard

130
00:06:53,790 --> 00:06:55,960
because you weren't able
to pay your tuition, right?

131
00:06:56,040 --> 00:06:59,540
Yes. He accepted me
despite how stubborn I was.

132
00:06:59,630 --> 00:07:01,210
And even though
I'm from a foreign land,

133
00:07:01,290 --> 00:07:02,790
he treated me
like one of his own.

134
00:07:02,880 --> 00:07:04,790
Mr. Glen really is
the best, huh?

135
00:07:08,750 --> 00:07:11,170
I think we should probably
go soon, Anne.

136
00:07:11,250 --> 00:07:12,210
[ANNE]
Right.

137
00:07:12,290 --> 00:07:13,540
Is something wrong?

138
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:17,130
Mr. Glen values tradition.

139
00:07:17,540 --> 00:07:20,580
Everyone here looks up to him
and respects what he says.

140
00:07:21,000 --> 00:07:22,000
It makes me wonder

141
00:07:22,080 --> 00:07:25,630
whether problems can be hard
to see because you care.

142
00:07:26,170 --> 00:07:28,420
If only everyone's feelings
could be united

143
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:30,130
toward the same goal.

144
00:07:30,210 --> 00:07:32,540
Well, there are some things

145
00:07:32,630 --> 00:07:34,880
that nobody
can do anything about,

146
00:07:34,960 --> 00:07:36,040
but in those cases,

147
00:07:36,130 --> 00:07:38,380
people tend to put their wishes
into sugar confections,

148
00:07:38,460 --> 00:07:39,380
don't they?

149
00:07:40,710 --> 00:07:42,920
Now, let's go eat some dinner.

150
00:07:43,000 --> 00:07:44,500
[laughs]

151
00:07:51,830 --> 00:07:54,580
[ANNE]
Everyone, gather around please.

152
00:07:54,670 --> 00:07:55,880
What's that for?

153
00:07:55,960 --> 00:07:58,880
I've missed the old feeling
of silver sugar in my hands.

154
00:07:58,960 --> 00:07:59,960
Orland, you and King

155
00:08:00,040 --> 00:08:01,790
should continue
with what you're working on.

156
00:08:01,880 --> 00:08:03,380
Nadir and Valentine,

157
00:08:03,460 --> 00:08:06,080
I'd like you to make something
with this to show me.

158
00:08:06,580 --> 00:08:08,500
I want to see what you can do.

159
00:08:28,040 --> 00:08:30,170
[ANNE]
Putting wishes into sugar.

160
00:08:30,250 --> 00:08:31,500
[sighs]

161
00:08:35,960 --> 00:08:38,580
- Amazing.
- It's impressive, right?

162
00:08:38,670 --> 00:08:41,080
It's Valentine's favorite shape.

163
00:08:41,170 --> 00:08:42,290
I don't get it,

164
00:08:42,380 --> 00:08:44,790
but he says it has something
to do with math.

165
00:08:46,750 --> 00:08:47,880
As for Nadir,

166
00:08:47,960 --> 00:08:49,630
the more detail
he puts into a piece,

167
00:08:49,710 --> 00:08:51,250
the more he likes it.

168
00:08:51,330 --> 00:08:54,000
Customers have fallen in love
with his work for that reason,

169
00:08:54,080 --> 00:08:55,630
so he gets requests often.

170
00:08:55,710 --> 00:08:58,630
I see. That makes sense.

171
00:08:58,710 --> 00:09:00,170
[door opens]

172
00:09:00,250 --> 00:09:01,630
[ANNE]
Mr. Glen.

173
00:09:02,380 --> 00:09:04,330
You should be lying down, sir.

174
00:09:04,420 --> 00:09:05,790
I'll be all right.

175
00:09:08,880 --> 00:09:11,380
The sweet scent of silver sugar.

176
00:09:12,080 --> 00:09:14,710
Coming here
always lifts my spirits.

177
00:09:14,790 --> 00:09:19,380
Orland, King, Valentine,
Nadir, and Anne.

178
00:09:19,460 --> 00:09:21,920
I say this as the head
of the Paige workshop,

179
00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:23,040
so listen.

180
00:09:24,210 --> 00:09:25,290
We've always rejected

181
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:28,170
the Holy Beginnings Festival
selection process.

182
00:09:28,250 --> 00:09:31,080
However, this year
we should participate.

183
00:09:32,040 --> 00:09:34,000
Are you sure
that's all right, sir?

184
00:09:34,080 --> 00:09:36,210
Only this once.

185
00:09:36,290 --> 00:09:37,790
If we are not chosen,

186
00:09:37,880 --> 00:09:39,920
then we will never
take part in it again.

187
00:09:40,630 --> 00:09:43,880
What's more, I'll ask you
to leave this place

188
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:46,500
and that fairy
will remain here with me.

189
00:09:47,580 --> 00:09:49,710
You're right, it's true
that I have chosen you

190
00:09:49,790 --> 00:09:52,460
to be our Head of Artisans,
which means

191
00:09:52,540 --> 00:09:55,790
you've taken on the
responsibilities that entails.

192
00:09:55,880 --> 00:09:56,880
[ANNE]
Yes, sir.

193
00:09:56,960 --> 00:09:58,750
As the Head of Artisans,

194
00:09:58,830 --> 00:10:00,830
I can promise you

195
00:10:00,920 --> 00:10:02,630
that I'll give it everything
I've got.

196
00:10:17,080 --> 00:10:19,420
When I did that...

197
00:10:20,250 --> 00:10:21,250
[ANNE]
Challe.

198
00:10:23,710 --> 00:10:25,330
[CHALLE]
Have you finished all your work?

199
00:10:26,790 --> 00:10:29,210
I have something
that I wanted to tell you.

200
00:10:30,790 --> 00:10:33,630
Mr. Glen just came and told us
that we're participating

201
00:10:33,710 --> 00:10:35,750
in the selection process
this year.

202
00:10:36,830 --> 00:10:37,920
Although...

203
00:10:38,000 --> 00:10:39,040
What is it?

204
00:10:40,040 --> 00:10:42,130
If Paige isn't selected,

205
00:10:42,210 --> 00:10:45,290
then I will be forced
to give up my job here,

206
00:10:45,380 --> 00:10:47,750
and he's not going to give
your wing back to me.

207
00:10:48,540 --> 00:10:51,710
Even so, I agreed to do it

208
00:10:51,790 --> 00:10:54,170
because I think it's necessary
for the workshop.

209
00:10:55,130 --> 00:10:56,710
That's why.

210
00:10:57,460 --> 00:11:00,500
[CHALLE] If you deem it
necessary, then it's fine.

211
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:02,040
[ANNE]
Are you sure?

212
00:11:02,130 --> 00:11:04,670
If we don't end up being chosen,
you'll be...

213
00:11:05,250 --> 00:11:07,460
[CHALLE] You asked me
to trust you and wait,

214
00:11:07,540 --> 00:11:12,330
so no matter what happens,
that's what I intend to do.

215
00:11:12,960 --> 00:11:14,380
Oh, Challe.

216
00:11:15,630 --> 00:11:17,790
Thank you. I'm glad.

217
00:11:18,750 --> 00:11:21,500
And even if we don't succeed,
don't be afraid

218
00:11:22,380 --> 00:11:24,500
because one way or another,

219
00:11:24,580 --> 00:11:27,130
I'm going to retrieve
your wing for you.

220
00:11:27,210 --> 00:11:28,750
I'm not afraid at all.

221
00:11:30,290 --> 00:11:31,500
Good.

222
00:11:42,630 --> 00:11:44,830
[Mithril laughing]

223
00:11:44,920 --> 00:11:46,040
Sorry to startle you,

224
00:11:46,130 --> 00:11:48,380
but it seems I've just
remembered an urgent matter.

225
00:11:48,460 --> 00:11:49,630
I must take my leave.

226
00:11:49,710 --> 00:11:50,630
The two of you should stay

227
00:11:50,710 --> 00:11:52,330
and enjoy
this beautiful scenery.

228
00:11:52,420 --> 00:11:54,540
In places like this,
some people hug,

229
00:11:54,630 --> 00:11:56,460
hold hands, and even kiss,
if you can believe it.

230
00:11:56,540 --> 00:11:59,040
What?
Please stop it, Mithril Lid Pod.

231
00:11:59,130 --> 00:12:00,830
Anyhow,
have a good time, you two.

232
00:12:00,920 --> 00:12:02,540
Especially you, Anne.

233
00:12:02,630 --> 00:12:04,170
I don't need your advice.

234
00:12:04,250 --> 00:12:05,460
Not that I would need it.

235
00:12:05,540 --> 00:12:07,040
I have no idea
what he's talking about.

236
00:12:07,130 --> 00:12:08,540
Just forget he was even here.

237
00:12:08,630 --> 00:12:12,670
Wait, Mithril Lid Pod.
Please come back, I beg you.

238
00:12:15,750 --> 00:12:19,250
What kind of confection
would be worthy of the festival?

239
00:12:19,330 --> 00:12:20,960
Perhaps we should try a motif

240
00:12:21,040 --> 00:12:24,040
that includes the Twelve
Patron Saints or the King.

241
00:12:24,130 --> 00:12:27,080
Nah, I don't like big,
clunky things like that.

242
00:12:27,170 --> 00:12:28,210
It'd be tacky.

243
00:12:28,290 --> 00:12:31,790
Not just that, but statues
of the saints are boring.

244
00:12:31,880 --> 00:12:34,460
If we did a flower,
then we could use more color.

245
00:12:34,540 --> 00:12:36,290
Flowers in wintertime?

246
00:12:36,380 --> 00:12:38,500
If the founding king
is the standard--

247
00:12:38,580 --> 00:12:40,580
All right,
let's think some more.

248
00:12:40,670 --> 00:12:42,750
- If we make something big--
- That's enough now!

249
00:12:42,830 --> 00:12:43,790
[KING]
Huh?

250
00:12:43,880 --> 00:12:45,630
You're all only suggesting ideas

251
00:12:45,710 --> 00:12:47,630
that appeal to each of you
personally.

252
00:12:48,500 --> 00:12:51,290
Only one of us is going to work
on the project, yeah?

253
00:12:52,040 --> 00:12:54,920
Right, I should've realized.
Please pardon my rudeness.

254
00:12:55,420 --> 00:12:58,710
You've got it all wrong, King.
Anne's going to make it.

255
00:12:58,790 --> 00:13:00,080
Wait. Seriously?

256
00:13:00,170 --> 00:13:01,960
Actually, I'm not.

257
00:13:03,540 --> 00:13:04,920
Now historically,

258
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:06,880
the Paige Workshop
has assigned each commission

259
00:13:06,960 --> 00:13:08,880
to a single sugar artisan,

260
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:11,080
but I've been thinking
of changing our procedure.

261
00:13:11,170 --> 00:13:13,040
- Changing?
- That's right.

262
00:13:13,130 --> 00:13:15,920
The five of us will come up
with an idea together,

263
00:13:16,000 --> 00:13:17,790
and then we can all
bring it to life.

264
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:19,790
All five of us?

265
00:13:19,880 --> 00:13:22,000
I highly doubt
that everyone's perspectives

266
00:13:22,080 --> 00:13:23,250
will be aligned.

267
00:13:23,330 --> 00:13:26,500
Moreover,
why do they need to align?

268
00:13:26,580 --> 00:13:27,790
I'm glad you asked.

269
00:13:28,210 --> 00:13:30,330
If we make a piece
we all approve of,

270
00:13:30,420 --> 00:13:33,960
then it's sure to feature
everyone's best attributes.

271
00:13:34,040 --> 00:13:36,500
Chances are,
if the five of us like it,

272
00:13:36,580 --> 00:13:39,420
then the other people
are sure to like it as well.

273
00:13:39,500 --> 00:13:41,670
[ORLAND]
If such a thing exists.

274
00:13:41,750 --> 00:13:44,210
We'll never know
until we try to find it, right?

275
00:13:44,290 --> 00:13:46,130
But where do we begin?

276
00:13:46,210 --> 00:13:48,130
We can sit and chatter
all we want.

277
00:13:48,210 --> 00:13:50,290
It doesn't mean
we'll agree on anything.

278
00:13:51,130 --> 00:13:52,710
I mean, our ideas
and preferences

279
00:13:52,790 --> 00:13:54,330
are all completely different.

280
00:13:54,420 --> 00:13:55,920
I understand.

281
00:13:56,000 --> 00:13:59,000
So then, what themes
have they selected in the past?

282
00:13:59,080 --> 00:14:00,790
What sort of pieces won?

283
00:14:01,710 --> 00:14:04,130
Maybe that information
would give us some hints.

284
00:14:04,750 --> 00:14:06,170
That reminds me.

285
00:14:08,080 --> 00:14:10,630
[ELLIOTT] These are the journals
of previous masters.

286
00:14:10,710 --> 00:14:12,710
It's not the sort
of reading material

287
00:14:12,790 --> 00:14:14,250
I'd ever go through. [laughs]

288
00:14:14,330 --> 00:14:15,420
Why is that?

289
00:14:15,500 --> 00:14:17,000
Trust me, you'll see.

290
00:14:17,080 --> 00:14:19,080
Anyway,
I've got some things to do.

291
00:14:19,170 --> 00:14:20,420
I'll be back tomorrow.

292
00:14:20,500 --> 00:14:23,290
If you're still at it,
I'll try to help. Mm, yeah.

293
00:14:23,960 --> 00:14:26,540
All right,
let's all start reading.

294
00:14:26,630 --> 00:14:29,000
If we split it,
we'll be done in no time.

295
00:14:29,080 --> 00:14:31,000
[KING]
I'm not so sure about that.

296
00:14:33,170 --> 00:14:34,630
A secret language?

297
00:14:34,710 --> 00:14:36,500
So, these journals are very old,

298
00:14:36,580 --> 00:14:39,540
as are all the speech patterns
and they're hard to read.

299
00:14:39,630 --> 00:14:41,500
I can't understand a word of it.

300
00:14:41,580 --> 00:14:44,210
[NADIR] Neither can I.
Even if I died trying.

301
00:14:44,290 --> 00:14:46,210
- I can read them.
- Hm?

302
00:14:46,290 --> 00:14:48,000
I can manage as well.

303
00:14:48,080 --> 00:14:50,750
So can I. Only us three?

304
00:14:50,830 --> 00:14:52,960
The selection is less
than two weeks away.

305
00:14:53,040 --> 00:14:54,210
Is there time?

306
00:14:54,290 --> 00:14:56,170
All we can do is divide them up.

307
00:14:56,250 --> 00:14:58,290
Three is better than none.

308
00:14:58,380 --> 00:14:59,630
- Let's begin.
- Mm-hm.

309
00:15:00,500 --> 00:15:01,580
Thank you.

310
00:15:01,670 --> 00:15:03,710
- Sorry.
- So am I.

311
00:15:03,790 --> 00:15:05,080
[ANNE]
It's all right.

312
00:15:05,880 --> 00:15:08,500
Ooh, that must be challenging
to read.

313
00:15:08,580 --> 00:15:10,460
It is, do you know
how to read it?

314
00:15:10,540 --> 00:15:11,540
No, I don't.

315
00:15:12,040 --> 00:15:14,420
Understanding that would require
a quality education

316
00:15:14,500 --> 00:15:16,250
and a great deal of studying.

317
00:15:16,880 --> 00:15:17,880
I know.

318
00:15:17,960 --> 00:15:20,540
Perhaps young Miss Bridget
would have some success with it.

319
00:15:20,630 --> 00:15:23,080
Speaking of which,
I haven't seen her around.

320
00:15:23,170 --> 00:15:25,250
Apparently,
she won't leave her room at all.

321
00:15:25,330 --> 00:15:28,330
[NADIR] Really? Fresh air
would do her good, though.

322
00:15:28,420 --> 00:15:29,630
I agree.

323
00:15:30,580 --> 00:15:32,380
- [crickets chirping]
- [knock on door]

324
00:15:34,130 --> 00:15:34,960
[BRIDGET]
Who's there?

325
00:15:35,790 --> 00:15:37,170
Challe?

326
00:15:37,250 --> 00:15:39,420
Sorry, no, it's Anne.

327
00:15:40,580 --> 00:15:42,670
Miss Bridget, there's something
that I was hoping

328
00:15:42,750 --> 00:15:44,580
to get your help with, so I...

329
00:15:50,880 --> 00:15:52,330
[BRIDGET]
My help?

330
00:15:52,420 --> 00:15:53,710
Um, yes.

331
00:15:54,420 --> 00:15:55,540
You see, we've been trying

332
00:15:55,630 --> 00:15:57,710
to read the previous masters'
journals,

333
00:15:57,790 --> 00:15:59,540
but they're hard to follow.

334
00:15:59,630 --> 00:16:01,920
So, I thought
if we could have you help us,

335
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:03,670
then perhaps it would be...

336
00:16:04,330 --> 00:16:05,420
[snickers]

337
00:16:05,500 --> 00:16:07,210
If I agreed
to such a silly thing,

338
00:16:07,290 --> 00:16:09,250
would Challe come
and pay me a visit?

339
00:16:09,330 --> 00:16:12,170
Well, that decision
would be up to Challe.

340
00:16:12,250 --> 00:16:14,290
I can't say one way or another.

341
00:16:15,080 --> 00:16:17,710
Fine, then if Challe
doesn't come to see me,

342
00:16:17,790 --> 00:16:19,920
I'm not helping you.

343
00:16:20,920 --> 00:16:22,790
I understand.

344
00:16:22,880 --> 00:16:25,250
Oh, just one more thing. Here.

345
00:16:25,330 --> 00:16:26,250
What?

346
00:16:27,080 --> 00:16:29,460
I was thinking of you,
and I made this.

347
00:16:29,540 --> 00:16:31,040
It's for you.

348
00:16:33,080 --> 00:16:34,790
[BRIDGET]
You made this for me?

349
00:16:35,790 --> 00:16:37,080
I don't want it.

350
00:16:37,170 --> 00:16:39,540
You're free to break it
and dispose of it.

351
00:16:40,500 --> 00:16:42,920
You know well
that I'm not going to do that.

352
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,130
That's why I can't accept it.

353
00:16:45,210 --> 00:16:46,630
I'll never accept it!

354
00:16:52,670 --> 00:16:53,670
[ANNE]
Orland?

355
00:16:56,540 --> 00:16:58,040
What were you doing in the hall?

356
00:16:58,790 --> 00:16:59,790
What about you?

357
00:17:00,580 --> 00:17:01,630
I don't know.

358
00:17:02,210 --> 00:17:04,420
I wanted to give this
to Miss Bridget.

359
00:17:04,500 --> 00:17:06,210
I thought if I did,
she might help us

360
00:17:06,290 --> 00:17:08,170
with making sense
of the journals.

361
00:17:08,250 --> 00:17:10,540
[ORLAND]
You are genuine to a fault.

362
00:17:10,630 --> 00:17:13,330
She'd never accept that,
nor would she help you.

363
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:16,540
[ANNE] But you were bringing
her this one as well,

364
00:17:16,630 --> 00:17:17,710
weren't you?

365
00:17:17,790 --> 00:17:19,790
That's just a simple apology.

366
00:17:19,880 --> 00:17:21,080
What for?

367
00:17:22,960 --> 00:17:23,790
[sighs]

368
00:17:25,130 --> 00:17:26,750
I'm the one
who stole Challe's wing

369
00:17:26,830 --> 00:17:28,790
and put it on Mr. Glen's pillow.

370
00:17:28,880 --> 00:17:29,710
[gasps]

371
00:17:29,790 --> 00:17:33,380
Really? It was you who did that?
But what do you stand to gain?

372
00:17:33,960 --> 00:17:36,460
I thought no one knew
about that hiding spot.

373
00:17:36,540 --> 00:17:38,170
They simply don't remember.

374
00:17:40,960 --> 00:17:41,880
Years back,

375
00:17:41,960 --> 00:17:44,790
when we were young children,
we would play together.

376
00:17:45,380 --> 00:17:47,330
It's been a long time,
but she showed me

377
00:17:47,420 --> 00:17:50,420
where that hiding spot was
in the fireplace.

378
00:17:50,500 --> 00:17:51,500
[ANNE]
I see.

379
00:17:51,580 --> 00:17:54,080
[ORLAND] Bridget aspired
to become a sugar artisan,

380
00:17:54,170 --> 00:17:55,710
so we were comrades.

381
00:17:56,790 --> 00:17:59,420
She said that was why
we should share our secrets.

382
00:17:59,500 --> 00:18:01,920
As a child,
she was cheerful and sweet...

383
00:18:03,170 --> 00:18:06,500
but the person she is now
disgusts me.

384
00:18:07,380 --> 00:18:09,330
I can barely look at her,

385
00:18:09,420 --> 00:18:11,540
and she's been so cruel
to Challe,

386
00:18:11,630 --> 00:18:12,920
so I stole the wing.

387
00:18:13,830 --> 00:18:15,460
Do you think
Miss Bridget changed

388
00:18:15,540 --> 00:18:17,040
because she was forbidden

389
00:18:17,130 --> 00:18:19,630
to become a sugar artisan
like she wanted?

390
00:18:20,040 --> 00:18:21,880
We're both girls, and I can.

391
00:18:21,960 --> 00:18:23,670
You'd think the daughter
of a school master

392
00:18:23,750 --> 00:18:25,130
would have that freedom.

393
00:18:26,710 --> 00:18:29,670
Well, if the child of a master
becomes a sugar artisan,

394
00:18:29,750 --> 00:18:31,580
they inherit the workshop.

395
00:18:32,500 --> 00:18:35,290
Being in charge of a school
is already difficult,

396
00:18:35,380 --> 00:18:36,920
it's even harder as a woman.

397
00:18:38,290 --> 00:18:39,830
And you know our situation.

398
00:18:40,790 --> 00:18:42,460
Mr. Glen didn't want
to leave his daughter

399
00:18:42,540 --> 00:18:44,540
in such an impossible position.

400
00:18:44,630 --> 00:18:46,130
He couldn't bear the thought.

401
00:18:48,170 --> 00:18:49,380
[ANNE]
Once again,

402
00:18:49,460 --> 00:18:52,080
everyone's feelings
are disconnected.

403
00:18:52,170 --> 00:18:53,790
It's making things worse.

404
00:18:53,880 --> 00:18:54,920
[MITHRIL]
There are some things

405
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:57,210
that nobody
can do anything about,

406
00:18:57,290 --> 00:18:58,330
but in those cases,

407
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:00,630
people tend to put their wishes
into sugar confections,

408
00:19:00,710 --> 00:19:01,750
don't they?

409
00:19:01,830 --> 00:19:03,460
I understand.

410
00:19:03,540 --> 00:19:05,750
All a person can do is wish.

411
00:19:09,330 --> 00:19:11,040
[BRIDGET]
You made this for me?

412
00:19:14,080 --> 00:19:15,420
Tell me something.

413
00:19:15,500 --> 00:19:17,460
That sugar confection,

414
00:19:17,540 --> 00:19:19,630
you made it
for Miss Bridget, right?

415
00:19:20,670 --> 00:19:23,500
Your creations always have
a certain strength to them,

416
00:19:23,580 --> 00:19:24,750
but when it's for Miss Bridget,

417
00:19:24,830 --> 00:19:27,170
you can make lovely things
like this.

418
00:19:28,710 --> 00:19:31,040
If it's in the service
of others.

419
00:19:32,250 --> 00:19:34,420
According to what we've learned
from the journals,

420
00:19:34,500 --> 00:19:36,500
the confections made
at the Paige Workshop

421
00:19:36,580 --> 00:19:39,710
sometimes didn't align well
with the kingdom's religion,

422
00:19:39,790 --> 00:19:41,170
which led to conflicts.

423
00:19:41,250 --> 00:19:44,040
That's why they started doing it
by selection instead.

424
00:19:44,130 --> 00:19:45,500
What's the bottom line?

425
00:19:45,580 --> 00:19:47,170
Are you saying
that we should craft something

426
00:19:47,250 --> 00:19:49,630
that caters to the kingdom's
religion this time?

427
00:19:51,250 --> 00:19:52,380
Not at all.

428
00:19:52,460 --> 00:19:55,000
It should be something
that we all want to make

429
00:19:55,080 --> 00:19:56,500
to honor Mr. Glen.

430
00:19:56,580 --> 00:19:57,460
[all gasp]

431
00:19:58,040 --> 00:20:00,210
He wants to protect
the Paige Workshop.

432
00:20:00,290 --> 00:20:01,830
That's why he agreed
to take part

433
00:20:01,920 --> 00:20:04,080
in the selection for this year.

434
00:20:04,170 --> 00:20:07,290
So, whatever we make,
we should make it for him.

435
00:20:07,380 --> 00:20:10,420
Something that'll keep him
and the workshop going strong.

436
00:20:10,920 --> 00:20:13,500
When Mr. Glen finally sees
our creation on display

437
00:20:13,580 --> 00:20:15,630
at the Church
of Saint Lewiston Bell,

438
00:20:15,710 --> 00:20:18,920
I want it to be something
that he can really be proud of.

439
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,040
Let's all give it our best.

440
00:20:21,130 --> 00:20:22,710
But what should we make?

441
00:20:23,210 --> 00:20:24,960
Now that I think of it,

442
00:20:25,040 --> 00:20:27,170
every year,
Mr. Glen hopes for snow

443
00:20:27,250 --> 00:20:29,000
on the night of the festival.

444
00:20:29,080 --> 00:20:32,630
He says that snowfall can lead
us all into a brand-new world.

445
00:20:32,710 --> 00:20:35,790
I've heard that snow
is one of his favorite motifs.

446
00:20:35,880 --> 00:20:37,040
He'd love that.

447
00:20:37,540 --> 00:20:39,750
So Mr. Glen favors snow.

448
00:20:39,830 --> 00:20:42,080
I think we have our answer.

449
00:20:42,170 --> 00:20:44,040
Let's create something
like that.

450
00:20:44,790 --> 00:20:47,130
[HAL]
Help! Someone, come quickly!

451
00:20:48,040 --> 00:20:50,170
Anyone, please! Hurry!

452
00:20:50,250 --> 00:20:51,080
[all panting]

453
00:20:51,170 --> 00:20:52,380
That sounded like Hal.

454
00:20:54,960 --> 00:20:56,960
Oh. Mr. Collins!

